   220	wt(b4)=1,  wt(b3)=t+1,  wt(u_i)=i  (2<=i<t).
   221	```
   222	
   223	Every monomial `b3^a b4^b product u_i^c_i` in `T_(t,k)`, for `k>0`, obeys
   224	
   225	```text
   226	(t+1)a+b+sum_(i=2)^(t-1)i c_i=4t+1-k.                (2.14)
   227	```
   228	
   229	For `k=0`, the same rule holds for the nonconstant part and the extra term is
   230	`-c`.  Hence, with `W=4t+1-k`,
   231	
   232	```text
   233	deg_b4 <= W,
   234	deg_b3 <= floor(W/(t+1)),
   235	deg_(u_i) <= floor(W/i),
   236	total degree <= W.                                   (2.15)
   237	```
   238	
   239	Reduction by `H_t` makes every coefficient affine in `y`.  Before unit
   240	division the coefficients are in `Z[t,y]`; after the explicitly listed
   241	divisions they are in `Q(t)+Q(t)y`.  Clearing the finite indexed denominator
   242	set in Section 3 therefore gives literal polynomials in `(t,y)`.  The support
   243	bound, before cancellations, is
   244	
   245	```text
   246	[z^W] 1/((1-z)(1-z^(t+1)) product_(i=2)^(t-1)(1-z^i)). (2.16)
   247	```
   248	
   249	No assertion that every allowed monomial occurs is needed or made.
   250	
   250	
   251	## 3. Denominators, resultants, and split safety
   252	
   253	This section is **PROVED-HERE** by exact algebra and the executable
   254	`denominator_audit.py`.  For a linear class `ad+b`,
   255	
   256	```text
   257	Res_y(H_t,ad+b)=4q^2(3b^2-a^2(t+1)),                 (3.1)
   258	(ad+b)^(-1)=3(b-ad)/(3b^2-a^2(t+1)).                 (3.2)
   259	```
   260	
   261	The elementary inverses used in (2.3)--(2.12) are
   262	
   263	```text
   264	y^(-1)=6q((t+1)-d)/((t+1)(3t+2)),
   265	g^(-1)=6q^3(2(t+1)-3d)/(t(t+1)e(4t+1)),
   266	p_(2t+1)^(-1)=6q^2(3qd-(t+1))/(t(t+1)e(4t+1)).       (3.3)
   267	```
   268	
   269	For `n=t-j>=1`,
   270	
   271	```text
   272	3B_C^2-A_C^2(t+1)=-(3t+2)^3 F_C(t,t-n),
   273	F_C(t,t-n)=
   274	 3n^4+24n^3t+42n^3+66n^2t^2+146n^2t+119n^2
   275	 +72nt^3+238nt^2+234nt+104n
   276	 +27t^4+134t^3+223t^2+136t+32.                     (3.4)
   277	```
   278	
   279	Every term on the last two lines is nonnegative for `t>=2,n>=1`, and the
   280	constant part is positive.  For `n=q-j>=1`,
   281	
   282	```text
   283	3B_Q^2-A_Q^2(t+1)=-(t+1)(3t+2)^2 F_Q(t,q-n),
   284	F_Q(t,q-n)=n^2+4nt+6n+4t^2-3 >= 12.                 (3.5)
   285	```
   286	
   287	Thus the primitive leading classes and their polynomial resultants are
   288	
   289	| diagonal class | resultant with `H_t` | roots in its admissible integer range |
   290	|---|---|---|
   291	| `y` | `(t+1)(3t+2)` | none for `t>=2` |
   292	| `3d+2(t+1)` (the class of `g`) | `12q^2(t+1)(4t+1)` | none for `t>=2` |
   293	| `L_C` | `-4q^2(3t+2)^3 F_C(t,j)` | none for `t>=2, 1<=j<t` by (3.4) |
   294	| `L_Q` | `-4q^2(t+1)(3t+2)^2 F_Q(t,j)` | none for `t>=2, t<=j<=2t` by (3.5) |
   295	| `3qd+(t+1)` | `-12q^2(t+1)(3t+2)(4t+1)` | none for `t>=2` |
   296	
   297	The additional residual-axis coefficient used in Section 6 has primitive
   298	linear class
   299	
   300	```text
   301	A_b d+B_b,
   302	A_b=27t^3-30t^2+t-2,   B_b=6t^3+13t^2-3t+2,
   303	```
   304	
   305	and exact resultant
   306	
   307	```text
   308	-4q^2(t-2)(3t-1)^2(3t+2)(27t^3+17t^2+t+2).          (3.6)
   309	```
   310	
   311	It is nonzero for every integer `t>=3`: every factor is then positive except
   312	the displayed leading minus sign.  Its sole admissible boundary zero is
   313	`t=2`, which is treated factorwise in Section 6.
   314	
   315	The scalar factors in the actual `p_j` contribute only their squares to a
   316	quadratic resultant.  Their complete rational denominator list is
   317	
   318	```text
   319	2, 3, 6, q, q^2, q^3, t, e, t+1, 3t+2, 4t+1,
   320	m+1 (0<=m<t), 2m+1 (0<=m<=2t),
   321	4t-2j+1 in the ranges of (2.10),(2.11), F_C, F_Q.    (3.7)
   322	```
   323	
   324	The axis formula adds only `3t-1` to this denominator list.
   325	
   326	The only integer roots among the unindexed linear factors are `t=0` (the
   327	factor `t`) and `t=-1` (the factor `t+1`); the other displayed linear roots
   328	are nonintegral.  The indexed odd factor `4t-2j+1` cannot be zero and is
   329	positive in its stated ranges.  Equations (3.4)--(3.5) exclude all admissible
   330	roots of `F_C,F_Q`.  Thus the specialization-exception set for integer
   331	`t>=2` is empty.  The infinite split set `t=3s^2-1` is not put into a
   332	denominator: (3.1) proves the pivot is a unit on both factors.
   333	
   334	## 4. Controls for the recurrence
   335	
   336	The new Singular driver performs (2.1)--(2.13) directly and checks every
   337	affine pivot row before substitution.  Its exact output agrees, with the sign
   338	(1.3), with every terminal coefficient in the banked JSON at `t=2,3,4,5`.
   339	The `t=5` source hash is independently authenticated by the charged status
   340	record.  At `t=6` the driver emitted all twelve exact characteristic-zero rows
   400	```text
   401	d=22y-6,       A_5=Q[d]/(d^2-2),
   402	```
   403	
   404	the five rows at bands `5,...,9`, with `b4=1`, were made primitive in the
   405	declared ring
   406	
   407	```text
   408	ring R=(0,d),(b3,q2_0,q3_0,q4_0),dp;  minpoly=d2-2;
   409	```
   410	
   411	Singular `modStd(I,1)` returned the reduced basis `G[1]=1` in 13.10 seconds,
   412	with empty stderr.  The local library documents exactness flag `1` as computing
   413	a standard basis "for sure" and invokes its final exact verification.  This is
   414	therefore **PROVED-HERE in characteristic zero**, and supersedes the charged
   415	timeout (which used a less favorable coefficient presentation).
   416	
   417	### 5.2 Why the proposed affine triangle is not yet present
   418	
   419	In the residual auxiliaries the top rows are nonlinear: for `t=3,4,5` their
   420	total degrees range respectively through `4--5`, `5--6`, and `6--8`.  Modular
   421	subsystem audits on one `H_t` fibre give the following zero-dimensional lengths
   422	after the first `t-1` rows; only adding the final row produces `[1]`:
   423	
   424	| `t` | ascending bands | descending bands |
   425	|---:|---:|---:|
   426	| 3 | 10 | 8 |
   427	| 4 | 57 | 44 |
   428	| 5 | 298 | 224 |
   429	
   430	Removing any one top row at `t=3,4,5` also leaves a nonzero zero-dimensional
   431	ideal.  Thus none of these records supplies the requested induction "one
   432	original row introduces one auxiliary with a unit affine coefficient".  A
   433	nonlinear triangular change may exist, but it has not been derived.  Calling
   434	the final Gröbner reduction a uniform triangle would violate the indexed-
   435	recurrence guardrail.
   436	
   437	## 6. `RESIDUAL-ZERO`
   438	
   439	At `b4=0`, (2.14) proves every positive-band row vanishes at the residual
   440	origin.
   441	
   442	### 6.1 A uniform `b3`-axis calculation
   443	
   444	Set all `u_2,...,u_(t-1)` to zero as well.  The weight induction (2.8)--(2.9)
   445	then leaves exactly
   446	
   447	```text
   448	C=X^(t-1),
   449	U=X^(2t+1)+a b3 X^t,       b2=0,
   450	a=-(6dt-3d+9t^2+2t-1)/(6t(3t-1)).                  (6.1)
   451	```
   452	
   453	Indeed, a solved high variable of weight `j` can be nonzero on this axis only
   454	when `j` is a multiple of `wt(b3)=t+1`; in the high range the sole possibility
   455	is `u_(t+1)`.  Its affine pivot equation gives the displayed `a`; the final
   456	weight `2t+1` cannot occur, so `b2=0`.  Substitution of (6.1) in the indexed
   457	arrays (2.3)--(2.7), and extraction at weight `2t+2`, gives for the stored sign
   458	`R=-E_t`
   459	
   460	```text
   461	[X^(2t-1)]R = lambda_b b3^2,
   462	lambda_b = t(3t+1)(A_b d+B_b)/
   463	           (12(2t+1)^2(3t-1)^2(3t+2)).              (6.2)
   464	```
   465	
   466	This coefficient extraction is an identity in `Q(t)[d]/(3d^2-t-1)`, not a
   467	fit to fixed values.  Resultant (3.6) proves `lambda_b` is a unit for every
   468	integer `t>=3`, on both factors at every split index.  Consequently the image
   469	of the positive-row ideal on the `b3`-axis has radical `(b3)`.  This is only a
   470	coordinate-axis statement: it does **not** imply a power of `b3` belongs to
   471	the original multivariable ideal.
   472	
   473	### 6.2 Exact fixed-`t` radical certificates
   474	
   475	Exact standard bases give the following pure-power containments in the
   476	positive-band ideal `I_(t,+)`:
   477	
   478	| `t` | coefficient algebra | basis size | checked powers lying in `I_(t,+)` |
   479	|---:|---|---:|---|
   480	| 3 | `Q[y]/(H_3)` | 4 | `b3^3, u2^6` |
   481	| 4 | `Q[y]/(H_4)` | 17 | `b3^4, u2^8, u3^6` |
   482	| 5 | `Q[y]/(H_5)` | 63 | `b3^5, u2^13, u3^9, u4^7` |
   483	| 6 | `Q[y]/(H_6)` | 253 | `b3^5, u2^16, u3^11, u4^8, u5^7` |
   484	
   485	Each reduction was zero in the displayed characteristic-zero algebra.  Since
   486	all generators vanish at the origin and a power of every residual variable
   487	lies in the ideal, these checks prove exactly
   488	
   489	```text
   490	sqrt(I_(t,+))=(b3,u_2,...,u_(t-1))  for t=3,4,5,6.   (6.1)
   491	```
   492	
   493	Here and below this displayed equality is renumbered `(6.3)`; the literal
   494	text is retained in the executable logs only by band name.
   495	
   496	The `t=5,6` instances are new exact promotions.  They remain fixed-integer
   497	proofs, not an induction.  On both `t=6` fibres modulo `1009`, the weaker
   498	uniform-looking augmented chain also holds: after adjoining successively
   499	`b3,u5,...,u_(j+1)`, the reduction of `u_j^7` is zero.  This is
   500	**MEASURED-MODULAR** evidence for exponent `t+1`; no symbolic leading
   501	coefficient has been obtained.
   502	
   503	There is a useful correction at `t=2`.  The positive rows are not literally
   504	all zero in `A_2`: the stored band-three row at `b4=0` is
   505	
   506	```text
   507	28(5y-1)b3^2/625
   508	```
   509	
   510	for `R=-E`.  It is zero on the `y=1/5` factor and a unit times `b3^2` on the
   511	`y=2/5` factor.  Therefore the proposed radical equality is still false in
   512	the product algebra, while the band-zero row is a unit on both factors and
   513	closes that chart exactly.
   514	
   515	The universal statement remains
   516	
   517	```text
   518	OPEN[RESIDUAL-ZERO-UNIFORM]: prove (6.1) for every t>=3. (6.2)
   519	```
   520	
   521	Equivalently, replace the two references in this line by `(6.3)` and `(6.4)`:
   522	the open statement is the all-`t` version of the fixed radical equality.
   523	
   524	The missing step is now precise: exhibit, by an index induction, `b3^N` in
   525	`I_(t,+)` and then a power of `u_j` modulo
   526	`(b3,u_(t-1),...,u_(j+1))`, with each coefficient's norm nonzero.  The
   527	finite exponents above show that guessing a sharp pure-power exponent from
   528	`t=3,4,5` is unsafe (`b3^5`, not first `b3^6`, already occurs at `t=6`).
   529	
   530	## 7. Assembly and exact theorem status

